      "text": "To determine how many times Terrell must lift the 15-pound weights to lift the same total weight as he does with the 20-pound weights, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the total weight Terrell lifts with the 20-pound weights.\n2. Determine how many times he must lift the 15-pound weights to achieve the same total weight.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the total weight lifted with the 20-pound weights. Terrell lifts two 20-pound weights, and he does this 12 times. The total weight lifted is:\n\\[ 2 \\times 20 \\times 12 = 480 \\text{ pounds} \\]\n\nNext, we need to find out how many times Terrell must lift the 15-pound weights to lift a total of 480 pounds. Let \\( x \\) be the number of times he lifts the 15-pound weights. The total weight lifted in this case is:\n\\[ 15 \\times 15 \\times x = 225x \\text{ pounds} \\]\n\nWe set this equal to the total weight lifted with the 20-pound weights:\n\\[ 225x = 480 \\]\n\nTo find \\( x \\), we solve the equation:\n\\[ x = \\frac{480}{225} = \\frac{64}{30} = \\frac{32}{15} \\approx 2.1333 \\]\n\nSince Terrell can only lift weights in whole numbers, we round up to the next whole number because he needs to lift the weights a whole number of times to achieve at least 480 pounds. Therefore, Terrell must lift the 15-pound weights 3 times.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{3} \\]",
